**Shutdown contract (DEN-3175).** `SIGTERM` leaves readiness *first*, then drains. Draining
before deregistering means new work keeps arriving into a process that is trying to leave.
`Stopped` records why — `Drained` vs `GraceExpired` — because a log that only says "stopped"
cannot tell a clean drain from a timeout.

**Invariants pinned by tests, not just happy paths:** readiness cannot be regained while
draining; repeated `SIGTERM` does not shorten the grace period; `SIGTERM` during startup is
not lost.

**Lease safety.** `heartbeat_interval` must be strictly less than `lease_duration`, enforced
at startup — a config failure is a startup failure, never a silent default. Default is a
third of the lease so one dropped renewal is survivable.

**Three managers, one constraint.** `TimeoutStopSec` (systemd), `ExitTimeOut` (launchd) and
`WaitToKillServiceTimeout` (Windows SCM) must all exceed `shutdown-grace-seconds`, or an
active drain is killed mid-job.

**No credentials as flags** — argv is visible in process listings. `.cli-flags.toml` is the
only option schema, parsed by flags-2-env.

Verified: `cargo fmt --check` · `cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings` · 9/9 tests pass.
